---

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r3
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a downed physical interface on R3, not a hung R9. R3's eth0 (IP 39.1.1.1/8, segment J) is in kernel state DOWN — confirmed by `ip link show eth0` returning `<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>` with no `UP`/`LOWER_UP` flags, and by FRR itself reporting "eth0 is down / OSPF not enabled on this interface." This is the only link connecting R3 to R9, since R9's only routed connection is eth0 on the same J segment.

R9 is entirely healthy: its eth0 is UP (`<UP,BROADCAST,RUNNING,MULTICAST>`), FRR is running, OSPF is correctly configured for all three prefixes (19.0.0.0/8, 39.0.0.0/8, 90.0.0.0/8), and it has elected itself DR on segment J — but it sees zero neighbors because R3's side of the link is down. With no OSPF adjacency, R9 cannot flood its LSAs into the domain, so R1 and the rest of the topology have no routes to 19.0.0.0/8, 39.0.0.0/8, or 90.0.0.0/8, which explains the reported flapping/disappearance of those prefixes. R3's own OSPF neighbor table confirms the absence of any entry for R9.

The most likely cause is that R3's eth0 was inadvertently shut down (`ip link set eth0 down`) during the maintenance window, or the Kathara container's startup script left it in the down state.

Root cause: R3's eth0 was left in link-down state after the maintenance window, severing the only path between R3 and R9 and suppressing R9's prefixes from the OSPF domain.

Corrective fix: On R3, run `ip link set eth0 up`. This will restore the L2 link, allow R3 and R9 to form an OSPF Full adjacency, and immediately re-advertise 19.0.0.0/8, 39.0.0.0/8, and 90.0.0.0/8 throughout the topology. No action on R9 is needed.
=== END ===